## ══════════════════════════════════════════
## 13. LIFECYCLE HOOKS — USAGE RULES
## ══════════════════════════════════════════

With signals, most lifecycle hooks become unnecessary. Use only when truly required.

| Hook | Use in | Reason |
|------|--------|--------|
| `ngOnInit` | `CatalogComponent`, `FilmDetailComponent`, `AboutComponent` | Set breadcrumbs; read route param; load film |
| `ngAfterViewInit` | `AutofocusDirective` | DOM is guaranteed ready — safe to call `.focus()` |
| `ngOnDestroy` | Not needed here | Signals clean up automatically; no subscriptions to cancel |
| `ngOnChanges` | **Avoid** | Use `input()` + `computed()` instead — reactive by nature |
| `ngDoCheck` | **Never** | Fires every CD cycle — extreme performance cost |
| `ngAfterContentChecked` | **Never** | Same — extreme performance cost |

**Rules:**
- Always declare the interface: `implements OnInit`, `implements AfterViewInit`
- NEVER declare signals inside `ngOnInit` — always at field level
- NEVER derive state inside a lifecycle hook — use `computed()`
- NEVER use `ngOnDestroy` just to call `markForCheck()` — OnPush + signals handles it

